<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gim Man-deok</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kim Man-deok, Man-deok halmang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gim Man-deok was a Korean merchant of Joseon from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju Island</a> who used her fortune to feed the island in a famine. Orphaned young, she became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisaeng">gisaeng</a>; at 22 she had her name removed from the gisaeng registry, regained her yangmin status and opened an inn for merchants and a commission agency for trade between Jeju and the mainland, becoming by 50 one of the two richest people in Joseon. When famine struck Jeju from 1792 and 170,000 people died of starvation, she imported and distributed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, saving 18,000 lives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a> granted her wish to visit the capital and Mount <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geum-river">Geumgang</a>, though Jeju women had been forbidden to leave the island for 200 years; she left her belongings to the poor.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some won lasting fame: the painter Sin Saimdang, mother of the philosopher Yi I; the gisaeng poet Hwang Jini; and the merchant <b>Kim Man-deok</b>, who gave her rice to the starving.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
